"health_atlas_atria_compare_caval" = "Superior caval opening";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_caval_detail" = "The superior vena cava opens high in the right atrium. The inferior vena cava opens below in the same smooth-walled venous region.";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_fossa" = "Fossa ovalis";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_fossa_detail" = "This shallow oval depression is on the septal wall of the right atrium. It is a remnant of the fetal foramen ovale, not an open passage in this adult view.";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_pectinate" = "Right pectinate ridges";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_pectinate_detail" = "These muscle ridges fan out into the right atrial appendage from the terminal crest, which separates the ridged wall from the smooth venous region.";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_pulmonary" = "Pulmonary vein openings";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_pulmonary_detail" = "Four openings are shown in the back of the left atrium, two from each lung. They admit blood returning from the lungs.";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_selector" = "Atrial cutaways";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_smooth" = "Smooth left atrial wall";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_smooth_detail" = "Most of the left atrial lining is smooth, including the posterior wall between the pulmonary vein openings. Pectinate ridges are confined to the left atrial appendage.";
"health_atlas_atria_compare_view" = "Opened atria compared side by side: right atrium on the left, left atrium on the right. These are separate cutaways.";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_calyx" = "Minor calyx";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_calyx_detail" = "This cup surrounds the papilla and receives urine from its openings. It belongs to the drainage passages beyond the collecting ducts.";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_cortical" = "Cortical collecting duct";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_cortical_detail" = "Connecting tubules from different nephrons feed the same cortical collecting duct. Two separate nephron outlets join the shared path shown here.";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_medullary" = "Medullary collecting duct";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_medullary_detail" = "Collecting ducts descend through the renal pyramid. In the inner medulla they converge into larger channels as they approach the papilla.";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_papillary" = "Papillary duct";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_papillary_detail" = "A papillary duct, also called a duct of Bellini, is the final collecting channel. It opens at the tip of the papilla into a minor calyx; one outlet is shown here.";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_tree_selector" = "Collecting path";
"health_atlas_collecting_ducts_tree_view" = "Collecting ducts from cortex to papilla";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_aortic" = "Aortic hiatus";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_aortic_detail" = "The aorta passes behind the diaphragm between the crura, under the median arcuate ligament. It does not pierce the muscle or tendon.";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_caval" = "Caval opening";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_caval_detail" = "The inferior vena cava passes through an opening in the right side of the central tendon.";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_costal" = "Costal muscle";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_costal_detail" = "Fibers attach along the inside of the lower ribs and their cartilages and run inward to the central tendon.";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_crus" = "Right crus";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_crus_detail" = "This longer right-hand bundle anchors the diaphragm to the upper lumbar spine. In this view from below, it appears on the left of the image.";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_esophageal" = "Esophageal hiatus";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_esophageal_detail" = "The esophagus passes through a muscular opening behind the central tendon. Fibers mainly from the right crus loop around the opening.";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_selector" = "Inferior surface";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_tendon" = "Central tendon";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_tendon_detail" = "The pale, three-lobed sheet lies where the surrounding diaphragm muscle fibers converge. It moves with the muscle during breathing.";
"health_atlas_diaphragm_inferior_view" = "Diaphragm from below, with the front at the top";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_connector" = "Connecting tubule";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_connector_detail" = "This short segment carries fluid from the distal convolution into the collecting system. The collecting duct also receives fluid from other nephrons.";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_convolution" = "Distal convolution";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_convolution_detail" = "This coiled segment stays in the cortex. Its lumen is continuous with the ascending limb, not with the nearby glomerular capillaries.";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_junction_selector" = "Distal junctions";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_junction_view" = "Distal tubule at the corpuscle and collecting junction";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_lining" = "Cuboidal lining";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_lining_detail" = "A single layer of cuboidal epithelial cells surrounds the distal tubule lumen. These cells lack the dense brush border of the proximal tubule, so the lumen has a clearer edge.";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_macula" = "Macula densa";
"health_atlas_distal_tubule_macula_detail" = "This patch of tightly packed cells sits near the end of the thick ascending limb beside the vascular pole of the glomerulus. It lies just upstream of the distal convoluted tubule.";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_aponeurosis" = "Calf aponeurosis";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_aponeurosis_detail" = "Fibers from both heads attach to a broad tendinous sheet. It narrows and joins the soleus contribution to form the Achilles tendon.";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_calcaneal_insertion" = "Calcaneal insertion";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_calcaneal_insertion_detail" = "The Achilles tendon carries both gastrocnemius heads and the soleus to the back of the calcaneal tuberosity, the prominent part of the heel bone.";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_lateral_origin" = "Lateral head origin";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_lateral_origin_detail" = "The lateral head starts just above the lateral femoral condyle and forms the outer bulge of the calf.";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_medial_origin" = "Medial head origin";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_medial_origin_detail" = "The medial head starts at the back of the femur just above the medial condyle. Its muscle belly forms the inner bulge of the calf.";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_posterior_attachments_selector" = "Calf attachments";
"health_atlas_gastrocnemius_posterior_attachments_view" = "Right gastrocnemius from behind, from the two origins above the knee to the shared tendon at the heel.";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_femoral_insertion" = "Gluteal tuberosity";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_femoral_insertion_detail" = "The lowest fibers attach to the gluteal tuberosity, a roughened ridge on the back of the upper femur below the greater trochanter.";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_iliac_origin" = "Iliac origin";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_iliac_origin_detail" = "Upper fibers arise along the posterior gluteal line and the adjacent outer surface of the ilium.";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_iliotibial_insertion" = "Iliotibial tract";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_iliotibial_insertion_detail" = "The upper fibers blend into the iliotibial tract, a thickened strip of fascia that continues down the outside of the thigh.";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_posterior_attachments_selector" = "Hip attachments";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_posterior_attachments_view" = "Right gluteus maximus from behind, with its origins on the pelvis and two insertions at the lateral hip.";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_sacral_origin" = "Sacral origin";
"health_atlas_gluteus_maximus_sacral_origin_detail" = "Medial fibers arise from the back of the sacrum and the side of the coccyx. Other fibers arise from the nearby sacrotuberous ligament.";
"health_atlas_hand_carpals" = "Carpal bones";
"health_atlas_hand_carpals_detail" = "Eight small bones in two rows form the base of the hand. Some meet the radius at the wrist.";
"health_atlas_hand_metacarpals" = "Metacarpals";
"health_atlas_hand_metacarpals_detail" = "Five metacarpal bones link the wrist to the digits and form the framework of the palm.";
"health_atlas_hand_palmar_detail" = "Hand bones from the palm";
"health_atlas_hand_phalanges" = "Finger phalanges";
"health_atlas_hand_phalanges_detail" = "Each of the four fingers has three phalanges, joined by joints that let the finger bend.";
"health_atlas_hand_pisiform" = "Pisiform";
"health_atlas_hand_pisiform_detail" = "This small round carpal sits on the palm side of the triquetrum near the little-finger edge.";
"health_atlas_hand_thumb" = "Thumb phalanges";
"health_atlas_hand_thumb_detail" = "The thumb has two phalanges rather than three. Its broad movement also depends on the joint at the base of its metacarpal.";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_pairs_selector" = "Paired gland map";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_pairs_view" = "Three pairs of major salivary glands, seen from the front";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_parotid" = "Parotid pair";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_parotid_detail" = "The largest pair sits in front of and below the ears. Each parotid duct crosses the cheek to open opposite an upper second molar.";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_sublingual" = "Sublingual pair";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_sublingual_detail" = "The smallest major pair lies just beneath the lining of the floor of the mouth, one gland on either side under the tongue. Several short ducts open along each sublingual fold.";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_submandibular" = "Submandibular pair";
"health_atlas_major_salivary_submandibular_detail" = "These glands lie mainly beneath the lower jaw. Each submandibular duct runs forward to an opening beside the lingual frenulum.";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_course" = "Course through the prostate";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_course_detail" = "The paired ducts enter at the base of the prostate and angle inward and forward toward the urethra, remaining separate as they cross the gland.";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_openings" = "Openings at the seminal colliculus";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_openings_detail" = "The two ducts empty through separate openings at the seminal colliculus, a raised landmark on the back wall of the prostatic urethra.";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_selector" = "Ejaculatory duct cutaway";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_union" = "Duct junction";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_union_detail" = "On each side, a seminal vesicle duct joins the ampulla of the vas deferens. The single narrow channel below each junction is that side’s ejaculatory duct.";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_urethra" = "Prostatic urethra";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_urethra_detail" = "This part of the urethra runs from the bladder neck through the prostate. The ejaculatory ducts empty into it rather than continuing as separate channels below the gland.";
"health_atlas_mdr_ej_view" = "Ejaculatory ducts · Posterior projection through the prostate";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_body" = "Body of the epididymis";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_body_detail" = "The narrower body runs behind and to the side of the testis. It contains many turns of the same continuous epididymal duct.";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_ductules" = "Efferent ductules";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_ductules_detail" = "These small channels lead from the rete testis into the head of the epididymis, where they feed into a single epididymal duct.";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_head" = "Head of the epididymis";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_head_detail" = "The broad head caps the upper pole of the testis and receives the efferent ductules.";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_selector" = "Epididymal duct";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_tail" = "Tail of the epididymis";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_tail_detail" = "At the lower pole of the testis, the coiled duct in the tail straightens and continues as the vas deferens, which turns upward.";
"health_atlas_mdr_epi_view" = "Epididymis · Posterolateral view with the testis partly cut away";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_blind" = "Blind upper end";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_blind_detail" = "The upper end of each seminal vesicle is closed. It lies behind the bladder and lateral to the end of the vas deferens.";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_coil" = "Coiled gland";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_coil_detail" = "Each vesicle is a coiled glandular tube. Its folded lining secretes fluid that contributes to semen.";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_outlet" = "Seminal vesicle duct";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_outlet_detail" = "This short outlet leaves the lower end of the gland and joins the ampulla of the vas deferens on the same side. The channel beyond the junction is an ejaculatory duct.";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_selector" = "Paired seminal vesicles";
"health_atlas_mdr_sv_view" = "Seminal vesicles · Posterior view at the bladder base";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_ampulla" = "Ampulla of the vas deferens";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_ampulla_detail" = "This widened end of the vas deferens lies medial to the seminal vesicle. It joins the duct of that vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct on the same side.";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_canal" = "Inguinal canal";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_canal_detail" = "The spermatic cord passes through this short passage in the lower abdominal wall. The vas deferens leaves the cord at the deep inguinal ring.";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_cord" = "Spermatic cord segment";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_cord_detail" = "The vas deferens ascends from the epididymal tail within the spermatic cord, alongside vessels and nerves.";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_pelvic" = "Pelvic segment";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_pelvic_detail" = "After entering the pelvis, the vas deferens runs along its side wall and turns toward the back and base of the bladder. It does not open into the bladder.";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_selector" = "Vas deferens route";
"health_atlas_mdr_vas_view" = "Vas deferens · One-sided route from the epididymis to the back of the bladder";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_choana" = "Choana";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_choana_detail" = "Each side of the nasal cavity has a posterior opening called a choana. It leads into the nasopharynx above the soft palate.";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_inferior" = "Inferior nasal concha";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_inferior_detail" = "The largest and lowest concha is a separate bone. Its lining helps warm and humidify passing air; the tear duct opens into the passage below it.";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_middle" = "Middle nasal concha";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_middle_detail" = "This curved shelf is part of the ethmoid bone. The passage beneath it receives drainage from several of the paranasal sinuses.";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_selector" = "Lateral nasal wall";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_superior" = "Superior nasal concha";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_superior_detail" = "The smallest of the three usual conchae projects from the upper back part of the lateral wall. Part of its surface lies in the olfactory region.";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_vestibule" = "Nasal vestibule";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_vestibule_detail" = "The vestibule sits just inside the nostril. Its skin-like lining contains coarse hairs that catch some larger particles.";
"health_atlas_nasal_wall_view" = "Side cutaway of the nasal cavity with the septum removed";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_bend" = "Duct bend in the head";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_bend_detail" = "In the broad head of the pancreas, the main duct curves toward the wall of the descending duodenum, where it usually joins the common bile duct.";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_papilla" = "Major duodenal papilla";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_papilla_detail" = "This small projection marks the outlet in the inner wall of the descending duodenum. In the common arrangement shown here, the two ducts share a short channel, the hepatopancreatic ampulla, just before the opening.";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_selector" = "Main duct cutaway";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_tributaries" = "Tail tributaries";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_tributaries_detail" = "Small ducts from the lobules converge near the narrow tail to form the beginning of the main pancreatic duct.";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_trunk" = "Main pancreatic duct (Wirsung)";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_trunk_detail" = "The main duct runs inside the pancreas. It receives smaller branches as it carries pancreatic juice from the tail through the body toward the head.";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_main_duct_view" = "Main pancreatic duct: tail to duodenum, anterior view";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_epiglottis" = "Epiglottis";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_epiglottis_detail" = "This leaf-shaped cartilage stands near the entrance to the larynx. During swallowing it folds back over the inlet, helping protect the airway.";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_laryngopharynx" = "Laryngopharynx";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_laryngopharynx_detail" = "The lowest region lies behind the larynx. It continues downward into the esophagus, while the laryngeal inlet opens toward the front.";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_nasopharynx" = "Nasopharynx";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_nasopharynx_detail" = "The upper pharynx lies behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. It carries air from the nose into the throat.";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_oropharynx" = "Oropharynx";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_oropharynx_detail" = "The middle pharynx lies behind the mouth and tongue, below the soft palate. Both air and swallowed material pass through it.";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_selector" = "Pharyngeal regions";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_soft_palate" = "Soft palate";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_soft_palate_detail" = "This movable extension behind the hard palate lifts during swallowing, separating the nasopharynx from the passage below.";
"health_atlas_pharynx_regions_view" = "Sagittal section through the three regions of the pharynx";
"health_atlas_prostate_apex" = "Prostate apex";
"health_atlas_prostate_apex_detail" = "The prostate narrows to its lower tip, where the urethra leaves the gland and continues toward the pelvic floor.";
"health_atlas_prostate_base" = "Prostate base";
"health_atlas_prostate_base_detail" = "The broad upper surface of the prostate meets the bladder neck, where the urethra enters the gland.";
"health_atlas_prostate_central_zone" = "Central zone";
"health_atlas_prostate_central_zone_detail" = "This wedge of glandular tissue surrounds the ejaculatory ducts. It is broad near the base and tapers toward the point where the ducts join the prostatic urethra.";
"health_atlas_prostate_peripheral_zone" = "Peripheral zone";
"health_atlas_prostate_peripheral_zone_detail" = "This glandular region forms much of the back and sides of the prostate. In this section it faces the rectum and extends down toward the apex.";
"health_atlas_prostate_sagittal_selector" = "Sagittal zones";
"health_atlas_prostate_sagittal_view" = "Prostate zonal cutaway, with the front of the body at left.";
"health_atlas_prostatic_urethra" = "Prostatic urethra";
"health_atlas_prostatic_urethra_detail" = "This segment of the urethra passes through the prostate from the bladder neck to the apex.";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_loop_bend" = "Loop of Henle bend";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_loop_bend_detail" = "The thin descending limb turns back toward the cortex at this hairpin in the medulla. The length of the loop varies among nephrons.";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_segment_selector" = "Tubule segments";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_segment_view" = "Renal tubules from corpuscle to collecting duct";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_thick_ascending" = "Thick ascending limb";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_thick_ascending_detail" = "The wider returning limb rises from the medulla to the cortex, passes close to the vascular pole of its own corpuscle, and leads into the distal convoluted tubule.";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_urinary_pole" = "Urinary pole";
"health_atlas_renal_tubules_urinary_pole_detail" = "At this opening, filtrate in Bowman's space enters the proximal tubule. The glomerulus is the capillary tuft inside the corpuscle, not part of the tubule.";
"health_atlas_sternum_angle" = "Sternal angle";
"health_atlas_sternum_angle_detail" = "The ridge where the manubrium meets the body sits at the level of the second costal cartilage.";
"health_atlas_sternum_body" = "Body of sternum";
"health_atlas_sternum_body_detail" = "The long middle part provides attachment points for the costal cartilages along both sides.";
"health_atlas_sternum_front_detail" = "Sternum from the front";
"health_atlas_sternum_manubrium" = "Manubrium";
"health_atlas_sternum_manubrium_detail" = "The broad upper part meets the two clavicles and the first costal cartilages.";
"health_atlas_sternum_notch" = "Jugular notch";
"health_atlas_sternum_notch_detail" = "The dip at the top of the sternum lies between the points where the clavicles meet the chest.";
"health_atlas_sternum_xiphoid" = "Xiphoid process";
"health_atlas_sternum_xiphoid_detail" = "The small lower tip is initially cartilaginous and can ossify with age. Its shape varies from person to person.";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_caruncle" = "Sublingual caruncle";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_caruncle_detail" = "One small bump sits on either side of the frenulum at its base. The submandibular duct opens on this bump; a larger sublingual duct may join it nearby.";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_fold" = "Sublingual fold";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_fold_detail" = "The gland raises a ridge in the lining of the mouth. Small sublingual ducts open along the ridge rather than all draining through one opening.";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_frenulum" = "Lingual frenulum";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_frenulum_detail" = "This midline fold connects the underside of the lifted tongue to the floor of the mouth and separates the paired caruncles.";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_gland" = "Sublingual gland bed";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_gland_detail" = "Each elongated gland lies under the floor-of-mouth lining beside the base of the tongue. The cutaway exposes the lobulated tissue beneath the fold.";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_rivinus" = "Ducts of Rivinus";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_rivinus_detail" = "These short ducts lead from the sublingual gland to separate openings along the fold. The illustration shows only some of them.";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_selector" = "Floor of mouth cutaway";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_view" = "Floor of the mouth: paired glands beneath the lifted tongue";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_wharton" = "Wharton duct";
"health_atlas_sublingual_floor_wharton_detail" = "This longer duct carries saliva from the submandibular gland forward to the caruncle. It passes on the inner side of the sublingual gland and is distinct from the short ducts of Rivinus.";
"health_atlas_tendons_achilles_midportion" = "Achilles tendon";
"health_atlas_tendons_achilles_midportion_detail" = "This segment of the Achilles tendon lies below the calf muscle bellies and above the heel attachment. The enlarged view looks across the tendon, rather than along it.";
"health_atlas_tendons_endotenon" = "Endotenon";
"health_atlas_tendons_endotenon_detail" = "This connective tissue runs between neighboring fascicles and separates their collagen bundles. It appears as narrow channels in the cross-section.";
"health_atlas_tendons_epitenon" = "Epitenon";
"health_atlas_tendons_epitenon_detail" = "The epitenon is the thin connective tissue layer immediately around the tendon proper. It forms the rim of the enlarged section.";
"health_atlas_tendons_fascicle" = "Collagen fascicle";
"health_atlas_tendons_fascicle_cross_section_selector" = "Tendon cross-section";
"health_atlas_tendons_fascicle_cross_section_view" = "Achilles tendon in place and an enlarged schematic cross-section showing how collagen bundles are organized.";
"health_atlas_tendons_fascicle_detail" = "A fascicle is a bundle of smaller collagen fibers that run mainly along the tendon. In cross-section, bundles appear as separate profiles.";
"health_atlas_vagina_anterior_wall" = "Anterior wall";
"health_atlas_vagina_anterior_wall_detail" = "The front wall of this fibromuscular canal lies behind the bladder base above and the urethra below.";
"health_atlas_vagina_posterior_fornix" = "Posterior fornix";
"health_atlas_vagina_posterior_fornix_detail" = "This recess lies between the back of the cervix and the upper vaginal wall. It reaches higher than the shallower recess in front of the cervix.";
"health_atlas_vagina_rugae" = "Vaginal rugae";
"health_atlas_vagina_rugae_detail" = "These transverse folds in the mucosal lining flatten as the vaginal wall stretches. The walls usually rest against each other along most of the canal.";
"health_atlas_vagina_sagittal_selector" = "Sagittal cutaway";
"health_atlas_vagina_sagittal_view" = "Vaginal canal in sagittal section, with the front of the body at left. The walls are shown slightly apart.";
"health_atlas_vaginal_opening" = "Vaginal opening";
"health_atlas_vaginal_opening_detail" = "The lower end of the vaginal canal opens into the vestibule, behind the separate opening of the urethra.";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_aortic" = "Aortic cusps";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_aortic_detail" = "The aortic valve has three semilunar cusps: right coronary, left coronary, and noncoronary. They close together at the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta.";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_mitral" = "Mitral leaflets";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_mitral_detail" = "The left inlet has two main leaflets. The deeper anterior leaflet meets the shallower, scalloped posterior leaflet along a curved closure line.";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_pulmonary" = "Pulmonary cusps";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_pulmonary_detail" = "Three pocket-shaped cusps meet at the center of the pulmonary valve, at the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk.";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_selector" = "Four valve faces";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_tricuspid" = "Tricuspid leaflets";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_tricuspid_detail" = "The right inlet has three main leaflets: anterior, posterior, and septal. The broad anterior leaflet is usually the largest.";
"health_atlas_valves_compare_view" = "Closed valve faces shown separately, not in one plane or at one moment: right heart on the left, left heart on the right; outflow valves above, inlet valves below. Viewed from the arteries or atria, respectively.";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_left_wall" = "Thick left free wall";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_left_wall_detail" = "The left ventricular muscle is thicker than the right free wall. It forms most of the ring around the rounded left cavity, completed by the septum.";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_right_wall" = "Thin right free wall";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_right_wall_detail" = "The outer muscle wall of the right ventricle is thinner than the left. In this section it forms the outer arc of the crescent.";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_selector" = "Ventricular short axis";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_septum" = "Muscular ventricular septum";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_septum_detail" = "This shared muscular wall separates the two ventricular cavities and forms the inner arc of the right ventricular crescent.";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_trabeculae" = "Right ventricular ridges";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_trabeculae_detail" = "Coarse muscular ridges, called trabeculae carneae, project into the right ventricular cavity from its inner surface.";
"health_atlas_ventricles_compare_view" = "Mid-ventricular cross-section looking from base toward apex: crescent-shaped right cavity at upper left and rounded left cavity at lower right.";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_basal" = "Fused basement membranes";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_basal_detail" = "In this thin region, the basement membranes of the alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium join, leaving very little tissue between the cells.";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_endothelium" = "Capillary endothelium";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_endothelium_detail" = "Flat endothelial cells line the blood side. Oxygen diffuses from alveolar air through the barrier into blood, while carbon dioxide moves in the opposite direction.";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_epithelium" = "Type I alveolar epithelium";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_epithelium_detail" = "Flat type I cells line most of the alveolar surface. Their thin extensions form the air-facing tissue layer of the barrier.";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_selector" = "Air–blood barrier layers";
"health_atlas_vi_barrier_view" = "Respiratory membrane · Enlarged thin region between alveolar air and blood";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_inlet" = "Pulmonary arteriole";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_inlet_detail" = "This small pulmonary arterial branch delivers oxygen-poor blood to the capillary network in the alveolar walls.";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_mesh" = "Septal capillary mesh";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_mesh_detail" = "Short, interconnected capillaries lie inside the thin walls shared by neighboring alveoli, not in the air spaces. The exposed mesh shows how much of the septum is available for gas exchange.";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_outlet" = "Pulmonary venule";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_outlet_detail" = "The capillary network drains into small venules. These collect oxygen-rich blood and carry it toward the pulmonary veins and left atrium.";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_selector" = "Alveolar capillary mesh";
"health_atlas_vi_pulmonary_view" = "Pulmonary capillaries · Section through alveoli with a septal surface exposed";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_bed" = "Capillary branch";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_bed_detail" = "These narrow channels exchange oxygen, nutrients, and wastes with the surrounding muscle tissue. Unlike pulmonary capillaries, they do not border an alveolar air space.";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_inlet" = "Feeding arteriole";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_inlet_detail" = "This small arterial branch supplies the capillary bed between muscle fibers.";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_outlet" = "Collecting venule";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_outlet_detail" = "Multiple small channels merge into this venule, which drains the muscle capillary bed toward larger veins.";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_selector" = "Skeletal muscle capillary bed";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_view" = "Systemic capillaries · Between skeletal muscle fibers, with one enlarged";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_wall" = "Continuous endothelium";
"health_atlas_vi_systemic_wall_detail" = "A single layer of flattened endothelial cells forms the lining of this muscle capillary. Its channel is narrow enough for red blood cells to pass in single file.";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_lumen" = "Venous lumen";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_lumen_detail" = "Blood flows through this inner channel. Veins generally have wider lumens and thinner walls than their accompanying arteries.";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_segment" = "Calf vein segment";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_segment_detail" = "This segment represents a vein running through the calf. Veins carry blood back toward the heart.";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_selector" = "Limb vein cutaway";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_valve" = "Paired valve leaflets";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_valve_detail" = "In this example of a valved limb vein, blood moving toward the heart pushes the two leaflets aside. If flow reverses, the leaflets come together to limit backflow. Not all veins have valves.";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_view" = "Representative calf vein · Longitudinal cutaway, toward the heart at top";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_wall" = "Vein wall";
"health_atlas_vi_vein_wall_detail" = "An inner lining faces the blood. Outside it lie a relatively thin layer of smooth muscle and a supporting layer of connective tissue.";
